	* mediawiki-discussion-tools.el
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--list-format)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--fit-columns)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--refit-on-resize): Grow the thread
	table's columns to fill the window.  Compute Title and Author widths
	from the window width instead of hard-coding 40/16, and refit on g
	and on window or frame resize via a buffer-local
	window-size-change-functions entry.  Narrow windows keep the old
	split.  The hook is installed by --ensure-hooks, which runs from the
	mode and from --refresh-table, because a buffer that was already in
	the mode does not gain a new buffer-local hook on reload.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools-next-link)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools-previous-link)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--link-run-start): Move between links in
	the thread view with TAB and S-TAB.  New commands, bound to TAB and
	<backtab> in both the view map and the link keymap, so they work
	wherever point is.  shr's own movement only works while point is on
	a link.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--render-html): Render comment links and
	images in the thread view.  Render each comment body with shr from
	the DiscussionTools HTML instead of stripping every tag, so links
	stay clickable and images are displayed.  Falls back to tag
	stripping when Emacs lacks libxml support, and keeps RET free by
	replacing shr's link keymap with the view's own.  A <base> element
	is injected into the parsed HTML, because `shr-insert-document'
	rebinds shr-base to nil and only sets it from <base>; without it the
	protocol-relative image URLs MediaWiki emits never got a scheme, the
	fetch failed and shr's grey placeholder was left in place.  The base
	is the wiki's own article URL, from a cached
	meta=siteinfo&siprop=general call, so relative ./File: links resolve
	to the article path too.  The trailing-space cleanup that could
	delete the image placeholder is gone.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools-open-link-at-point)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--wiki-title-from-url): New command on
	C-c C-o in the thread view.  Links to pages on the current site open
	with mediawiki-open; anything else goes to browse-url.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--render-replies): Open the comment at
	point in a web browser.  Attach each comment's DiscussionTools id to
	its rendered text as the mediawiki-comment-id property.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools-browse-comment-at-point)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--comment-id-at-point): New command on &
	in the thread view.  It builds <page url>#<comment id> and hands it
	to browse-url, so the browser lands on that comment instead of the
	top of the page.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools-resolve): Finish the discussion
	browser's status workflow and replace the planned archive scanning
	with a vanished-thread notice and search.  Replace the stub with a
	working command.  Prompts for an optional summary, appends
	{{Resolved|summary|~~~~}} indented under the thread's last comment,
	and refreshes the list and view buffers.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--resolved-anchors)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--mark-resolved): Detect {{Resolved}} by
	fetching the page wikitext once per refresh and matching it inside
	each section's byte range, so the list can show the checkmark
	without a per-thread fetch.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--priority-tiers)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--thread-priority): Sort by the plan's
	tiers (attention, watching, active, unanswered, resolved, stale)
	instead of putting unanswered first, with the two attention tiers
	reserved until per-comment data is fetched.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--thread-status): Return needs-attention
	when the logged-in user posted and someone answered afterwards, and
	watching when their comment is still last.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--flatten-replies)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--thread-contains-username-p)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--replied-after-username-p): Detect the
	user's own comments in a reply tree and whether anyone replied after
	them.
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--current-user)
	(mediawiki-discussion-tools--userinfo): Resolve the logged-in user
